# main.lume — how far does an `extend` travel?
#
#   kitchen/scale.lume    holds `interface Weighed` and two `extend`s,
#                         on `Int` and on `[Int]`
#   kitchen/pantry.lume   imports kitchen.scale                (one hop)
#   kitchen/recipe.lume   imports kitchen.pantry               (two hops)
#   main.lume             imports kitchen.recipe and kitchen.pantry
#                         — and NEVER imports kitchen.scale
#
# The README says: "An `extend` travels with the import, so a library ships
# its conformances and a consumer that imports it writes none of its own."
# The travel/ example proves the one-hop case. It does not say whether the
# conformance keeps travelling: is it a property of the program once any file
# has loaded scale.lume, or is it a property of *this file's* imports?
#
# Both answers are reasonable and they are very different to live with:
#   * program-wide, and `2050.kilos_text` below works, but a method appears on
#     `Int` in a file that cannot see where it came from, and adding an import
#     three files away can change what a method call means here;
#   * import-local, and `2050.kilos_text` is refused, which means a library
#     that wraps another library has to re-export conformances it cannot name
#     (there is no `pub extend` — the README says so explicitly: "it has no
#     name, so there is no `pub extend`"). That is a real hole.
#
# Meant to RUN, betting on the program-wide answer. Lines 55-56 and 60-61 are
# the bet; if they are refused, the error should say the conformance exists
# but is not in scope here, and name kitchen/scale.lume. Lines 48-50 and 64
# are the one-hop case and must work either way.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# cake (3 items)
# 2050
# flour: 1kg 500g; sugar: 0kg 250g; butter: 0kg 300g
# 2kg 50g
# true
# 2050
# 2kg 50g
# salt: 0kg 40g

import kitchen.recipe
import kitchen.pantry

def main:
  items = [pantry.item("flour", 1500), pantry.item("sugar", 250), pantry.item("butter", 300)]
  r = recipe.recipe("cake", items)

  # one hop: pantry.Item uses the conformance inside pantry.lume
  puts r.label
  puts r.weight
  puts r.lines

  # two hops: `kilos_text` and `heavy?` come from kitchen/scale.lume, which
  # this file does not import, applied to a plain `Int`
  total = r.weight
  puts total.kilos_text
  puts total.heavy?

  # the same again for the `extend [Int]`
  weights = [1500, 250, 300]
  puts weights.grams
  puts weights.kilos_text

  salt = pantry.item("salt", 40)
  puts salt.line
